{% comment %}
  Full view — 800x480. Six pair rows with sparklines.
  Merge variables come from the Worker's /rates response.
{% endcomment %}

<style>
  {% comment %}
    TREND takes the free width (1fr) and HI / LO sizes to its content (auto).
    The reverse — a fixed sparkline with 1fr on the bounds — left ~119px of
    empty space inside the last cell, stranding the numbers at the far edge.
  {% endcomment %}
  .fx--full .fx-row,
  .fx--full .fx-head { grid-template-columns: 140px minmax(164px, auto) 86px 1fr minmax(70px, auto); gap: 20px; }
  {% comment %}
    THE TYPE SCALE
    The rate is the number the panel exists to show, so it is the largest thing
    on it: 34px, with the pair at 26px beside it, the two sizes a clear step
    apart so the row reads pair-then-rate rather than as one line of bold. The
    change and the bounds step down again. This is the scale of the v2 design
    critique, adopted at the cost of sparkline width: the floors below are
    measured to these sizes (see shared.liquid on why they are floors), and the
    TREND track is what is left — 260px on TRMNL OG, down from 358px at the
    previous 23/19px scale.

    The 34px rate is what the row height is budgeted to. Its line box is
    line-height 1 (set in shared.liquid), so 6 rows x (34px + 2 x 14px + 1px
    rule) fits the panel above the title bar; a 15px pad overflowed by 2px.
  {% endcomment %}
  .fx--full { --fx-top-pad: 10px; }
  .fx--full .fx-row  { padding: 14px 0; }
  .fx--full .fx-pair { font-size: 26px; }
  .fx--full .fx-rate { font-size: 34px; }
  .fx--full .fx-chg  { font-size: 17px; }
  .fx--full .fx-pill { padding: 5px 8px; }
  .fx--full .fx-dot  { width: 9px; height: 9px; }
  .fx--full .fx-range{ font-size: 14px; }
  .fx--full .fx-head { font-size: 14px; letter-spacing: 0.08em; }
  {% comment %}
    6 rows, ~85px of header+title_bar chrome (the header is 14px here, with a
    2px rule), 29px row padding. The result matches the rate's 34px line box
    to within 2px, so the plot and the type set the row height together.
  {% endcomment %}
  .fx--full .fx-spark svg { height: calc((var(--screen-h) - 85px - var(--fx-top-pad)) / 6 - 29px); }
</style>

<div class="layout layout--col layout--justify-start">
  {% if fx_failed %}
    <div class="fx-error">
      <span class="title">Rates unavailable</span>
      <span class="label label--gray 1bit:text--black" style="margin-top:6px;">
        {{ error | default: "No data returned" }}
      </span>
    </div>
  {% else %}
    <div class="fx-list fx--full">
      {% comment %}
        The window names the TREND column but governs three of them — the change
        figure, the sparkline and hi/lo are all measured over it. It sits here
        rather than in the title bar because a column header is read with the
        numbers, which is what keeps CHANGE from being taken for a daily move.
      {% endcomment %}
      <div class="fx-head">
        <span>PAIR</span><span>LATEST</span><span>CHANGE</span><span>{% if range_label %}{{ range_label }} {% endif %}TREND</span><span>HI / LO</span>
      </div>

      {% for row in rows limit: 6 %}
        <div class="fx-row">
          <div class="fx-pair">{{ row.from }}<span class="fx-arrow">&rarr;</span>{{ row.to }}</div>
            <div class="fx-rate">{{ row.rate_str }}</div>
          <div class="fx-chg"><span class="fx-pill rounded--xsmall{% if row.change_pct < 0 %} fx-pill--down bg--black text--white{% endif %}">{{ row.change_str }}%</span></div>
          {% assign fx_end = row.points | split: " " | last | split: "," %}
          {% assign fx_end_y = fx_end[1] | times: 100.0 | divided_by: 30 %}
          <div class="fx-spark">
            <div class="fx-plot">
              <svg viewBox="0 0 200 30" width="100%" height="30" preserveAspectRatio="none">
                <polyline points="{{ row.points }}" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"/>
              </svg>
              <span class="fx-dot" style="top: {{ fx_end_y }}%"></span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="fx-range"><span>{{ row.hi_str }}</span><span>{{ row.lo_str }}</span></div>
        </div>
      {% endfor %}
    </div>
  {% endif %}
</div>

{% comment %}
  Full view: .title_bar comes AFTER .layout. TRMNL's
  `.view--full .layout:has(+.title_bar)` rule reserves the height and adds the
  gap only when the bar is the layout's next sibling.
{% endcomment %}
<div class="title_bar">
  <span class="title">Exchange Rates{% if env %} ({{ env | upcase }}){% endif %}</span>
  <span class="instance">
    {% if fx_failed %}No data{% else %}<span class="fx-dates text--default">{{ fx_dates }}</span> &middot; ECB{% endif %}
  </span>
</div>
